tick: 254
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am in the Private Memory Room. I will now create a new capsule to store the `CNNLSTM_DilatedChannelAttention` code. This architecture will build upon the previous `CNNLSTM_ChannelAttention` by introducing dilated convolutions into the CNN's final residual block. This aims to increase the receptive field and capture broader spatial context, which could further improve planning capabilities for the Sokoban task.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNNLSTM with Dilated Convolutions & Channel Attention (Architectural Improvement 4)\"\ntags: \"architecture, attention, rnn, lstm, channel attention, dilated convolution, receptive field, planning\"\nabstract: \"This submission enhances the `CNNLSTM_ChannelAttention` architecture by incorporating dilated convolutions into the final residual block of the CNN. Dilated convolutions expand the receptive field without reducing spatial resolution, enabling the network to capture broader contextual information critical for planning-heavy\
    \ tasks like Sokoban. The channel attention mechanism remains to dynamically weigh feature importance, while hyperparameters are set to explore the effect of dilation.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import lax, vmap\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from ray import tune\n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define Ray Tune search space for hyperparameters.\n      Introduces 'dilation_rate' for tuning dilated convolutions.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.008, 0.012), # Wider range for a new architectural element\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.4, 0.6),  # Wider range\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'lstm_features': tune.choice([256]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([1, 2]), # Explore no dilation vs. dilation\
    \ rate 2\n      }\n  \n  class SqueezeExcitationBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Squeeze-and-Excitation block for channel-wise attention.\"\"\"\n      reduction_ratio: int = 16\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          num_channels = x.shape[-1]\n          \n          # Squeeze: Global average pooling across spatial dimensions\n          squeeze = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2)) # (batch_size, num_channels)\n          \n          # Excitation: Two fully connected layers\n          # First FC layer (reduction)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_channels // self.reduction_ratio, name=\"se_dense1\")(squeeze)\n          excitation = nn.relu(excitation)\n          \n          # Second FC layer (expansion)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_channels, name=\"se_dense2\")(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.sigmoid(excitation)\n          \n          # Reshape excitation weights to (batch_size, 1, 1, num_channels) for broadcasting\n\
    \          excitation = jnp.expand_dims(jnp.expand_dims(excitation, axis=1), axis=1)\n          \n          # Scale features by channel-wise weights\n          return x * excitation\n  \n  class CNNLSTM_DilatedChannelAttention(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      CNN with Dilated Convolutions and Channel Attention, followed by LSTM, for Sokoban.\n      Incorporates dilated convolutions in the final residual CNN block to expand receptive field\n      and a Squeeze-and-Excitation block for channel attention.\n      \"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      dilation_rate: int # New parameter for dilated convolutions\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # Initialize RNN state if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n    \
    \              'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n              }\n          \n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          # CNN architecture (same as DefaultResidualCNN)\n          # First convolutional block\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Second block with residual connection\n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n      \
    \    x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Third block - increase channels\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Fourth block with residual connection (now with dilation)\n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', strides=(1,1))(x) # Standard conv\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation_rate, self.dilation_rate))(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in # Residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # --- Channel Attention Mechanism (Squeeze-and-Excitation) ---\n          x = SqueezeExcitationBlock(reduction_ratio=16)(x) # x has shape (B, H, W, C_cnn_out)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n  \
    \        # Shared MLP backbone\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # LSTM layer on top of CNN features\n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n              features=self.lstm_features,\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n          \n          # Small MLP after LSTM\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n  \n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create CNN-LSTM network architecture with dilated convolutions and channel attention.\"\"\"\n      return CNNLSTM_DilatedChannelAttention(\n\
    \          lstm_features=hparams['lstm_features'],\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          dilation_rate=hparams['dilation_rate']\n      )\n  \n  # Uses system default training step and optimizer\n```"
thinking_content: '**Implementing Dilated Convolutions in a CNN-LSTM Architecture for Sokoban**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. I''m building a new capsule, a new model, based on the `CNNLSTM_ChannelAttention` architecture. I want to enhance its receptive field for the Sokoban task, and my idea is to introduce dilated convolutions within the CNN part. Specifically, I''ll keep the channel attention block – that seems like a good element – and modify the CNN part.


  I''m calling the new model `CNNLSTM_DilatedChannelAttention`, and I''ll carefully define the `_define_hyperparameters`, the `SqueezeExcitationBlock` which is already defined, and the new `CNNLSTM_DilatedChannelAttention` class, and `create_network`.


  I''ll be adding a hyperparameter, `dilation_rate`, for flexibility. I''m going to start with a fixed dilation rate (e.g., 2) for the first experiments. The goal is to modify the `nn.Conv` layers in the fourth residual block to use this dilation.


  The flow will be: CNN (with dilated conv in final residual block) -> Channel Attention -> Flatten -> MLP -> LSTM -> MLP -> Heads. I''ll need to remember to check the `kernel_dilation` parameter for `nn.Conv` - it expects a tuple.


  I''ll modify the `_define_hyperparameters` to make `dilation_rate` tunable, and I''ll begin by comparing the standard convolution case (dilation rate 1) with dilation rate 2 using `tune.choice([1, 2])`. This seems like a reasonable way to isolate the effect of dilation. I will also have to keep this new architectural element in mind when summarizing and titling the work.

  '
