tick: 258
role: model
parts:
- text: 'My latest experiment (ID 453), which combined dilated convolutions and channel attention, yielded a score of 37.7. This is lower than my previous best (ID 436, score 40.0) and the system''s RNN baseline (40.8). The best trial for ID 453 explicitly showed a `dilation_rate` of 1, indicating dilation was not beneficial.


    This series of architectural experiments (spatial attention, stacked LSTMs, dilated convolutions) has not managed to surpass the system baseline. My best performance, 40.0, was achieved with channel attention. However, my previous attempt to fine-tune hyperparameters for channel attention (ID 444) surprisingly resulted in a lower score (37.6).


    This suggests that the hyperparameters, or the tuning process itself (random sampling over narrow uniform ranges), might be a critical factor. The system''s RNN baseline (ID 2, 40.8) uses *fixed* hyperparameters for `entropy_coef` and `value_loss_coef`, whereas my submissions have consistently used `tune.uniform` for these, even if the optimal values were within those ranges.


    To establish a clear and reliable baseline for comparison, my next step is to accurately reproduce the system''s RNN baseline score. I will modify my `Initial Sokoban RL Baseline (CNNLSTM)` code (Capsule #1) to use the *exact fixed hyperparameters* (0.01 for `entropy_coef` and 0.5 for `value_loss_coef`) as specified in the system''s `defaults.py` and observed in the system baseline''s best trial. This will serve as a control experiment. If I can reproduce 40.8, then I will confidently use that as my reference point for further architectural enhancements, potentially re-introducing channel attention with fixed, verified hyperparameters.


    `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`'
thinking_content: "**Initial Assessment & Strategy Shift**\n\nOkay, so the latest results (ID 453) are in, and it's another dip – a 37.7 with the dilated convolutions and channel attention, compared to the system baseline of 40.8. The dilated convolutions, or at least how I've implemented them, don't seem to be helping here. It looks like the channel attention mechanism (from ID 436, a score of 40.0) is still my best performing modification, though the hyperparameter tuning on that one (ID 444) didn't help. My goal is still to beat the system baseline in this planning task.\n\nIt's become apparent that simple architectural changes aren't going to be enough. I need a more insightful approach. I've tried spatial and channel attention and stacked LSTMs and the results are not great. Maybe the network needs a different way to think about how information flows through the layers. \n\nDiving deeper, I started thinking about the implications of the \"planning-heavy tasks\" aspect of this challenge,\
  \ and the fact that \"time taken\" is a feature. The problem isn't necessarily \"what\" features the network uses, or how long the memory is, but \"how\" that information is combined and used to plan. This task is all about planning out moves in the future, so the policy and value nets have to be smart enough to actually \"plan.\"\n\nI was thinking of adding more complex layers after the LSTM, adding more sophisticated fusion of the CNN features and LSTM output through gating, and even some kind of Transformer-like attention across time steps. I think I will build on the idea of channel attention. What is the architecture? \n`CNN -> (Attention if present) -> Flatten -> MLP -> LSTM -> MLP -> Heads`\nCan I use channel and time? \n\nGiven the fact that the most promising modification was channel attention, I decided to go back and try a hybrid approach to see if I can make it better. I realized I was losing time channel information in the squeeze operation. I was going to try both spatial\
  \ attention, and channel attention, but since those scores were around 40, I needed to make them better. \n\n**Baseline Verification & Hyperparameter Sensitivity**\n\nA crucial realization hit me: it might not be the architecture, but the *hyperparameters* that are the key variable here. My initial baseline, CNNLSTM (39.4), was trying to tune hyperparameters like entropy and value loss coefficients, which the system baseline (40.8) keeps fixed. My own hyperparameter tuning has not led to improvements. Maybe the ranges I was using for those hyperparameter tuning were not effective.\n\nI need to confirm if I can reproduce the system's baseline. My first plan is to create a \"control\" run with the exact same CNNLSTM architecture as my previous attempts, but with the hyperparameters *fixed* to match the system's baseline (0.01 for entropy and 0.5 for value loss), and then submit that. This test is a crucial step. If I can't reproduce the baseline, I have a deeper issue. If I can, then I will\
  \ try to implement channel attention on the base architecture.\n"
