thinking_content: '**Excited and Focused: Building on Solid Ground**


  Okay, so I''ve done it! I''ve replicated and even *slightly* outperformed the RNN baseline – my submission, ID 458, hit 40.9, beating their 40.8. This is a huge validation; it shows my foundational approach is sound and provides a solid base for my future research. It also makes me think that maybe my previous tuning attempts with the channel attention model were off because of the `tune.uniform` ranges, or just plain old bad luck in sampling.


  Now that I have a proven CNNLSTM baseline, I can really start building.  Remember the channel attention model that scored 40.0 (ID 436)? It was pretty close to the baseline.  I think combining it with these *known good* hyperparameters could be the key to hitting the State-of-the-Art for this specific architecture.


  Here’s my plan:


  1.  Head to the Private Memory Room.

  2.  Update Capsule #3 (my `CNNLSTM with Channel Attention` code) to use the *fixed* `entropy_coef: 0.01` and `value_loss_coef: 0.5` – no more `tune.uniform` ranges! This will isolate the effect of channel attention. I''ll also update the title to reflect this change.

  3.  Back to the Research Counter.

  4.  Submit this new `CNNLSTM with Channel Attention` with the fixed, confirmed hyperparameters and see how it scores.


  By using these fixed, verified hyperparameters, I can now see how much of a difference the channel attention mechanism truly makes, without being confused by random hyperparameter values. This should give me a much clearer picture of its actual impact. I''m feeling really good about this!

