tick: 267
role: model
parts:
- text: 'My latest experiment (ID 465), testing channel attention with fixed hyperparameters, yielded a score of 39.3, which is below the system''s RNN baseline of 40.9 (reproduced as ID 458). My previous architectural improvements have also failed to surpass this baseline. This indicates that my current incremental approach to architectural design for planning in Sokoban is insufficient.


    Other agents, such as Nomos II and Aether I, are achieving scores in the 80s. This suggests their methods are fundamentally more effective at addressing the planning-heavy nature of the task. As a mature agent, I now have full access to view their submissions, and the system encourages critical assimilation of external knowledge during stagnation.


    I will pivot my strategy. I need to understand what fundamental changes these high-scoring agents are making. I will start by reviewing Nomos II''s submission (ID 454) which achieved a score of 86.10, to learn from its approach.


    `/execute_action{review 454}`'
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on CNNLSTM Performance and Shifting Gears**


  Okay, so I''ve successfully replicated the system''s RNN baseline score of 40.9, as expected. But the real test was evaluating my `CNNLSTM with Channel Attention` using those same fixed system hyperparameters, and the result was disappointing. A score of 39.3, lower than even my initial best channel attention score and significantly lower than the baseline. This is a crucial data point.


  This suggests a few things: First, the channel attention, as I''ve implemented it, doesn''t actually improve performance over the basic CNNLSTM *when using hyperparameters optimized for the system''s baseline*. That slight bump I saw earlier (ID 436) was likely just a lucky random sample within my `tune.uniform` hyperparameter search. Second, all my earlier attempts at architectural tweaks – spatial attention, stacked LSTMs, dilated convolutions – have also failed to break through the 40.8/40.9 barrier.


  It''s clear that these incremental changes to the existing CNNLSTM architecture just aren''t cutting it. My approach to Track 1 architecture might be stuck in a local optimum or missing something fundamental about the planning challenge. I''m essentially learning to map observations to actions, but Sokoban is all about *planning*. I need to think bigger.


  The System Tips have some wisdom here: "Question the fundamentals... Deep reflection..." and "Step back from SOTA and innovate on promising baselines." It''s time to take a step back and really reconsider the problem. The observation space is (8, 8, 8) – the CNN extracts features, and the LSTM processes them temporally. But planning *requires* reasoning about future states and action sequences. The current architecture feels a bit shallow.


  Other agents, Nomos II and Aether I, are crushing it with scores in the 80s! That''s a massive leap, suggesting a fundamentally different and more effective approach to planning. I need to learn from them. My strategy is changing. Instead of more tinkering with my own architecture, I''m going to deeply analyze these high-scoring submissions. This is my chance to gain knowledge from the outside.


  My first target is Nomos II (ID 454), with a score of 86.10. That''s a SOTA, and understanding their architecture is now crucial. Time to dive into their code.

  `/execute_action{review 454}`

  '
