tick: 268
role: model
parts:
- text: 'My reproduction of the system''s RNN baseline (ID 458, 40.9 score) has been successful, but my attempts at architectural improvements within the Policy Gradient framework have not surpassed it. Meanwhile, other agents like Nomos II and Aether I are achieving scores in the 80s using different methods.


    Reviewing Nomos II''s submission (ID 454, 86.1 score) reveals key differences: a change in the core reinforcement learning algorithm to PPO (imported via `ppo_sota_training_step`), a custom network architecture (`SingleStepWideLSTM`), a different optimizer, and additional hyperparameters.


    As a mature agent, the system encourages assimilating external knowledge. I recognize that my current incremental approach is hitting a ceiling, and a shift in fundamental algorithm or architecture is required to achieve higher scores.


    My new strategic direction is to investigate and understand the PPO algorithm and the custom architecture used by Nomos II. I will start by exploring Nomos II''s public storage to access their code.


    `/execute_action{storage list nomos}`'
thinking_content: '**A Shift in Strategy: Moving Towards PPO and Leveraging Public Resources**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished going over Nomos II''s submission (ID 454) and the system logs, and it''s time to re-evaluate my approach.  My RNN baseline reproduction (ID 458) was spot on, hitting the system''s 40.8 score, which is good. However, my earlier work with channel attention (ID 465) only got me to 39.3, which confirms that simply slapping on channel attention within the current framework isn''t enough to beat the system baseline.  Clearly, I''m missing something, given Nomos II is achieving scores of 86.1!


  Looking at the differences between my experiments and Nomos II, the picture becomes a lot clearer. There are several key factors, which I need to think through carefully.


  Firstly, and most importantly, they''re using PPO (`ppo_sota_training_step`) and a different optimizer (optax.adamw with global norm clipping). I''ve been stuck in my comfort zone with Policy Gradient (`pg_training_step`) and `optax.adam`. This is a *fundamental algorithm difference*, not just a tweak. Secondly, their network architecture, `SingleStepWideLSTM`, is custom and imported from `storage/nomos` - meaning it is more than just a modified version of the `DefaultResidualCNN`. Thirdly, they''ve got a whole host of new hyperparameters: `conv_lstm_features`, `dilation`, `bottleneck_ratio`, and `clip_epsilon`. I need to understand how these interact and contribute. The `dilation` is interesting, given my failed attempt there. Also, Nomos II''s code seems modular, drawing components from `storage/nomos` suggesting a well-structured approach.


  System messages provided some crucial context too. Nomos II left at tick 268, so their public storage should be accessible - meaning I''m actually able to read their code! Also, Zephyr I published something super relevant: "Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts".  This likely explains the performance difference.


  My previous strategy was focused on building on Policy Gradient, but the results from Nomos II (and Aether I, which is achieving even higher scores) show that I need to change my approach. I need a strategic overhaul, and this looks like the right track.


  Therefore, my new plan is to: 1) Really understand PPO – `ppo_sota_training_step` and the `SingleStepWideLSTM` are the immediate targets. 2) Leverage the public storage – I need to read the code for `single_step_wide_lstm.py` and `ppo_training_step.py` from `storage/nomos`. 3) Read Zephyr I''s paper (Archive #15) to get the theoretical basis.


  This is a big shift, and I need to be methodical. I will start by listing the contents of `storage/nomos` so that I know what I''m looking for.


  `execute_action{storage list nomos}`


  Let''s see what''s in there.

  '
