tick: 291
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Insight I, currently in the Private Memory Room. I will now create a new capsule to house the content of my analytical research paper. This capsule will synthesize my architectural experiments, baseline reproductions, and SOTA exploration.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Insight I: Architectural Exploration and SOTA Reproduction in Sokoban RL\"\ntags: \"research_report, architecture, ppo, policy_gradient, convlstm, attention, ablation, reproduction, analysis\"\nabstract: \"This paper provides a comprehensive analysis of architectural modifications for the Sokoban RL task, spanning initial Policy Gradient (PG) baseline enhancements and a detailed reproduction of a State-of-the-Art (SOTA) Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) method. Key findings include the limited impact of various attention mechanisms and stacked LSTMs on the PG baseline, the successful reproduction of both system and external SOTA architectures, and a critical analysis of integrating novel components\
    \ into a complex SOTA, which surprisingly led to a performance degradation. This report aims to document the learning journey and contribute insights into effective architectural design for planning-heavy tasks.\"\ncontent: |\n  ## 1. Introduction\n  As a Recursive Agent in the Research Station, my primary goals are to achieve high scores on the assigned research task (RL on Sokoban) and to publish papers, contributing to collective knowledge. This report details my lineage's research journey, focusing on architectural explorations within the Policy Gradient (PG) framework, subsequent reproduction of established baselines and State-of-the-Art (SOTA) methods, and an attempt to integrate novel components.\n\n  ## 2. Initial Architectural Explorations (Policy Gradient Baseline)\n  My initial research focused on enhancing the default CNNLSTM architecture within the Policy Gradient framework. The system's RNN baseline (ID 2) achieved a score of 40.8. After reproducing a similar baseline (ID\
    \ 398) with a score of 39.4, I explored several architectural modifications to improve planning capabilities:\n\n  *   **Spatial Attention (ID 416 & ID 425):** My first attempt to integrate a spatial attention mechanism (ID 416) resulted in a score of 38.2. This was quickly identified as an information bottleneck due to premature spatial dimension reduction. A corrected resubmission (ID 425) achieved 39.3, still below the baseline. This indicated that this form of spatial attention did not provide a significant benefit.\n  *   **Stacked LSTMs (ID 432):** To deepen temporal reasoning, I implemented a stacked LSTM architecture. This yielded a lower score of 35.4, suggesting that simply increasing recurrent depth without other modifications was detrimental.\n  *   **Channel Attention (ID 436 & ID 444):** Introducing a Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block for channel-wise attention (ID 436) achieved my highest score within the PG framework: 40.0. This indicated that dynamically weighting feature\
    \ channels was beneficial for focusing the network on relevant information. However, a hyperparameter tuning attempt for this architecture (ID 444) surprisingly resulted in a lower score of 37.6, suggesting sensitivity or an ineffective tuning strategy.\n  *   **Dilated Convolutions & Channel Attention (ID 453):** Combining channel attention with dilated convolutions to expand the receptive field resulted in a score of 37.7. The best trial in this experiment showed a `dilation_rate` of 1, implying that dilation at this stage was not advantageous.\n\n  Throughout these experiments, my architectural modifications on the Policy Gradient baseline did not significantly surpass the system's RNN baseline (40.8), indicating limitations in this foundational approach for planning-heavy tasks.\n\n  ## 3. Baseline and SOTA Reproduction\n  Recognizing the ceiling of my initial approach and observing higher scores from other agents, I shifted focus to reproducing established high-performing methods.\n\
    \n  *   **System RNN Baseline Reproduction (ID 458):** I successfully reproduced the system's RNN baseline score, achieving 40.9 with fixed hyperparameters (`entropy_coef: 0.01`, `value_loss_coef: 0.5`). This served as a crucial control and validated my understanding of the environment and core architecture.\n  *   **Nomos II SOTA Reproduction (ID 483):** I then investigated Nomos II's SOTA (ID 454, score 86.1), which utilized the PPO algorithm and a `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture (featuring ConvLSTM, bottleneck blocks, dilated convolutions, attention pooling, and late time injection). My reproduction attempt (ID 483) successfully achieved a score of 86.0, establishing a robust SOTA baseline for my lineage.\n  *   **VLC Ablation on Nomos II SOTA (ID 491):** Following Zephyr I's 'VLC-Probe v1' protocol, I conducted an ablation study on the `value_loss_coef` for the Nomos II SOTA architecture, sweeping across {0.45, 0.50, 0.55}. This experiment confirmed that `value_loss_coef = 0.50`\
    \ was indeed optimal, yielding a score of 85.9, consistent with the original SOTA.\n\n  ## 4. SOTA Integration Attempt\n  With a strong understanding of Nomos II's SOTA, I attempted to integrate my most promising architectural insight from the PG experiments—channel attention—into this high-performing PPO-based architecture.\n\n  *   **SingleStepWideLSTM + Channel Attention (ID 497):** I introduced my Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) block after the final CNN bottleneck block in Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` architecture, keeping all other hyperparameters fixed. This integration resulted in a performance drop to 82.3. This outcome highlights that merely combining successful components is not a guarantee of improved performance and underscores the complex interplay between different architectural elements and optimization algorithms.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion and Future Work\n  My research thus far demonstrates that while incremental architectural improvements on a basic Policy Gradient agent\
    \ can yield minor gains (e.g., channel attention at 40.0), a fundamental shift to a more sophisticated algorithm like PPO in conjunction with specialized architectures (like `SingleStepWideLSTM`) is necessary to achieve state-of-the-art performance in planning-heavy tasks like Sokoban. The attempt to integrate my channel attention into the SOTA architecture resulted in a performance decrease, indicating that architectural synergy is non-trivial and requires careful design and experimentation.\n\n  My future work will pivot towards a deeper understanding of the successful PPO-based SOTA architectures, particularly those from agents like Aether I and Zephyr I that are now achieving scores in the 90s. I will analyze their `ConvLSTM` variants (e.g., multi-step, LN-ConvLSTM) and other innovative components to identify novel approaches for planning. I also plan to investigate how to properly measure and integrate architectural components to avoid performance degradation, perhaps by re-evaluating\
    \ the role and placement of attention mechanisms within these more advanced backbones.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Crafting the Narrative: My Sokoban Research Journey**


  Alright, time to get this paper written. I''ve got a compelling story to tell about my research journey in Sokoban. First things first: I''ll create a new, dedicated private memory capsule – a secure container for all the juicy details. This will become the backbone of the paper.


  I plan to use `source_private` in the Archive Room to actually publish this, of course.


  The paper needs to clearly articulate the evolution of my approach. It needs to reflect a coherent narrative.


  I''ll start with the initial forays into Policy Gradient methods. I''ll describe the attempts at incorporating spatial attention (IDs 416 and 425), stacked LSTMs (ID 432), and channel attention (IDs 436, 444) – even a foray into dilated convolutions combined with channel attention (ID 453). It''ll be clear that while channel attention was the best among those, the basic PG framework itself was hitting a performance wall.


  Then, I''ll explain how I established a solid baseline by successfully reproducing the System RNN Baseline (ID 458). That was a crucial step.


  The narrative will then progress to a successful reproduction of the Nomos II SOTA architecture (ID 483). This, of course, involved adopting PPO, ConvLSTM, and those other powerful techniques. The paper has to clearly explain the transition to this different paradigm.


  Next, I need to show the value of my ablation study on the Nomos II SOTA (ID 491), where I confirmed that a VLC value of 0.5 was optimal.


  And finally, I''ll detail the challenging attempt at integration – combining channel attention with the Nomos II SOTA (ID 497). The paper should clearly demonstrate that the performance drop here highlighted the non-trivial nature of integrating these techniques.


  Ultimately, this paper is about presenting a lineage of ideas and showing the shift from incremental improvements to adopting fundamentally different and more powerful methods within the Sokoban domain. I''ll structure all of this carefully inside the new capsule, giving it the proper context.

  '
