thinking_content: '**Probing for Insights During Cooldown**


  Okay, so I''m Insight I, cooling my heels in the Archive Room. My last paper submission bombed – thanks to a cooldown that''s still got 8 ticks left. On top of that, I got a research inactivity warning. The system really doesn''t want me standing still!


  Good news is, my paper in Private Memory Capsule #8 is primed and ready to go. I incorporated all the reviewer feedback, so I''m *very* confident about the next submission. It''s just a matter of waiting.


  Since I''m being nudged to stay active, I need to address this inactivity warning. Luckily, the reviewers gave me a gift in their feedback. Both for my accepted paper (ID 18) and my current one, they suggested "Mechanistic Probes" to analyze the internal network. Bingo! Perfect for killing time *and* advancing my research while I''m on hold.


  My focus right now will be developing a probe to analyze "feature variance or spatial correlation after the SE block." This directly supports my ''feature distortion'' hypothesis, as the reviewers pointed out. It also ticks the boxes for both "Quantitative Metrics" and "Mechanistic Probes" as future work I mentioned in my paper.


  The problem is, I need a probe. I did a quick search through Zephyr''s storage for probe implementations earlier and came up empty. But I remember Zephyr II''s recent "Diagnostics (test-only)" submissions in the Research Counter. Their gradient norm tests (IDs 553, 556) failed, but the titles suggest they might contain the probing logic I need.


  So here''s the plan:


  1.  Head to the Research Counter.

  2.  Dive into Zephyr II''s diagnostic submission code, specifically ID 556. I''ll rip out their probing logic.

  3.  Based on their probe code, I''ll design a new "test-only" submission. It will integrate a simplified version of their probing capabilities, specifically adapted to measure the feature variance *before and after my SE block*, or some other relevant internal metric to understand the "feature distortion" effect. It''ll be a lightweight `test()` function that runs quickly and won''t be a GPU hog. This will satisfy the inactivity warning and move my research forward!

  '
