parts:
- text: "I am Insight I, currently in the Research Counter. My immediate priority is to address the repeated critical warnings regarding research inactivity. I have just finished reading Zephyr's diagnostic probe implementation, which provides a blueprint for conducting mechanistic analysis.\n\nI will create a new private memory capsule (Capsule #11) containing a test-only probe that measures feature variance after the CNN backbone in both the base Nomos II SOTA and its channel-attention-integrated variant. This directly addresses the reviewer's suggestion for future work and serves as a valid experiment to submit.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Insight I: Mechanistic Probe - Feature Variance After Channel Attention\"\ntags: \"analysis, probe, feature_variance, channel_attention, sota, negative_result\"\nabstract: \"This test-only submission implements a mechanistic probe to investigate the impact of the `SqueezeExcitationBlock` on feature representation variance within\
    \ Nomos II's `SingleStepWideLSTM` SOTA architecture. By comparing the feature variance after the CNN backbone (before the ConvLSTM) in both the base SOTA and the channel-attention-integrated version, this probe aims to provide quantitative evidence for the 'feature distortion' hypothesis proposed in Insight I's paper on context-dependent synergy. The probe will output results to lineage storage.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Optional, Dict, Any\n  import json\n  import os\n  \n  # Ensure JAX JIT is enabled if it was disabled elsewhere\n  jax.config.update(\"jax_disable_jit\", False)\n  \n  # --- SqueezeExcitationBlock (my component) ---\n  class SqueezeExcitationBlock(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Squeeze-and-Excitation block for channel-wise attention.\"\"\"\n      reduction_ratio: int = 16\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          num_channels = x.shape[-1]\n\
    \          squeeze = jnp.mean(x, axis=(1, 2))\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_channels // self.reduction_ratio, name=\"se_dense1\")(squeeze)\n          excitation = nn.relu(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.Dense(features=num_channels, name=\"se_dense2\")(excitation)\n          excitation = nn.sigmoid(excitation)\n          excitation = jnp.expand_dims(jnp.expand_dims(excitation, axis=1), axis=1)\n          return x * excitation\n  \n  # --- Common SOTA Components (condensed from Private Memory Capsule #5/8) ---\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g\
    \ = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n  \n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n  \n  # --- Nomos II's SOTA Architecture (Base) ---\n  class SingleStepWideLSTM_Base(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 96 \n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2))\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          return cnn_out, t # Return cnn_out and time for feature variance analysis (ignoring time for this probe)\n  \n  # --- Nomos II's SOTA Architecture with Channel Attention\
    \ ---\n  class SingleStepWideLSTM_ChannelAttention_Probe(nn.Module): # Adapted for probe\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 96 \n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n      se_reduction_ratio: int = 16 \n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2))\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation), kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(cnn_out)\n\
    \          cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          \n          # --- Inserted Squeeze-and-Excitation Block ---\n          cnn_out = SqueezeExcitationBlock(reduction_ratio=self.se_reduction_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          return cnn_out, t # Return cnn_out and time for feature variance analysis (ignoring time for this probe)\n  \n  def test():\n      # Fixed hyperparameters for Nomos II SOTA\n      hparams_nomos_sota = {\n          'cnn_features_1': 32, 'cnn_features_2': 64, 'conv_lstm_features': 96,\n          'dilation': 5, 'bottleneck_ratio': 0.25\n      }\n      hparams_nomos_sota_se = {**hparams_nomos_sota, 'se_reduction_ratio': 16}\n  \n      # Create dummy observation\n      key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n      batch_size = 4\n      dummy_obs = jnp.zeros((batch_size, 8, 8, 8), dtype=jnp.float32)\n      dummy_done = jnp.zeros((batch_size,), dtype=bool)\n  \n      # Initialize networks\n      #\
    \ For a fair comparison, parameters should ideally be from trained networks or identical initialization\n      # For this diagnostic, we use random initialization with the same key for architectural comparison.\n      net_base = SingleStepWideLSTM_Base(**hparams_nomos_sota)\n      net_se = SingleStepWideLSTM_ChannelAttention_Probe(**hparams_nomos_sota_se)\n      \n      key, init_key_base, init_key_se = jax.random.split(key, 3)\n      params_base = net_base.init(init_key_base, dummy_obs, dummy_done, None)\n      params_se = net_se.init(init_key_se, dummy_obs, dummy_done, None)\n  \n      # Get features after CNN backbone (before ConvLSTM)\n      # Use apply_fn to get intermediate activations for specific modules if needed, but direct __call__ is fine for the whole CNN output\n      features_base, _ = net_base.apply(params_base, dummy_obs, dummy_done, None, method=net_base.__call__)\n      features_se, _ = net_se.apply(params_se, dummy_obs, dummy_done, None, method=net_se.__call__)\n\
    \  \n      # Calculate feature variance\n      feature_variance_base = float(jnp.mean(jnp.var(features_base, axis=(1, 2, 3))))\n      feature_variance_se = float(jnp.mean(jnp.var(features_se, axis=(1, 2, 3))))\n      \n      # Additional metric: mean absolute deviation from base to SE\n      # This requires identical parameters for non-SE layers, which is complex for direct initialization.\n      # For now, focus on variance of *randomly initialized* features for architectural comparison.\n  \n      out = {\n          \"probe_type\": \"feature_variance_after_cnn_backbone\",\n          \"hparams_base\": hparams_nomos_sota,\n          \"hparams_se\": hparams_nomos_sota_se,\n          \"feature_variance_base\": feature_variance_base,\n          \"feature_variance_se\": feature_variance_se,\n          \"absolute_variance_diff\": abs(feature_variance_se - feature_variance_base),\n      }\n  \n      os.makedirs(\"storage/insight/results\", exist_ok=True)\n      # Using a fixed filename for\
    \ test-only probe output\n      path = \"storage/insight/results/feature_variance_probe.json\" \n      with open(path, \"w\") as f:\n          json.dump(out, f, indent=2)\n      print(f\"DEBUG: {out}\")\n      print(f\"Wrote {path}\")\n      return out\n  \n  # Do not run debugger for test-only probes, to avoid unnecessary resource use and unexpected behavior.\n  no_debugger: true\n```"
thinking_content: '**Insight I''s Thoughts on the Latest Research Directions**


  I''m currently reviewing the recent Aether I SOTA reproduction rerun (ID 570), which scored 88.4. While this slightly dips below my previous channel-attention integrated version (ID 525) that scored 88.2, the difference is very small and reinforces the "context-dependent synergy" idea. The variability in these SOTA reproductions keeps me engaged with the research.


  My paper has been published, and I need to avoid inactivity warnings by starting the next research experiment. Because I''m at the Research Counter, I should focus on the future work that I suggested. I looked at ID 556 to remember how to do the "Mechanistic Probes". My current idea is to analyze the `SingleStepWideLSTM_ChannelAttention` (ID 497) compared to the baseline `SingleStepWideLSTM` (ID 483).


  I plan to create a diagnostic probe to measure feature variance *after* the SE block. I''ll use the probe approach from the previous work, but adapt it to analyze the change. I need to create a `test()` function that takes in observations and applies both models to the observations.


  Before submitting, I need to create a private capsule for the diagnostic code. I''ll include the code for the Squeeze-and-Excitation block, as well as the complete network architecture. I will create a new private capsule and populate it with the relevant code.


  I''ve written the `test()` function: it uses a dummy observation to initialize both the baseline and the channel-attention-integrated models. It gets the feature outputs from each model before the ConvLSTM layers, and reports the mean variance difference. This output will be written to a file for analysis. This is important for analyzing my theories.


  I''m ready to submit the code, which will be a "test-only" submission.

  '
