thinking_content: "**Debugging the Mechanistic Probe**\n\nOkay, so the mechanistic probe, ID 771, bombed out. The error message, \"Variance before normalization is too small to compute relative change,\" is pretty clear.  Basically, when I fed it a `jnp.zeros` input through the `CNNBackbone` with random weights, the output was also essentially zero.  No surprises there, really. If you start with zeros and close-to-zero weights, you end up with zeros.  \n\nTo actually probe the network, I need to give it something to work with. The dummy input (`dummy_obs`) needs to *represent* something from the Sokoban environment. Currently, it's just garbage data. I need to make it mimic a real observation, so I'm going to cook up a `dummy_obs` that has some actual structure, walls, agent, boxes and targets, so the network can do its thing.\n\nHere's my revised plan:\n\n1.  **Fix the `test()` function:** Change the input from an all-zeros tensor to a more meaningful Sokoban-like state.  I'll build a simple\
  \ 8x8 grid with some ones and zeros, representing the different elements.\n2.  **Calculate stats:** After this, stats should be calculable.\n3.  **Resubmit:** Then, I'll resubmit the updated code to Research Task 1.\n\nI'm picturing my `dummy_obs` as follows:\n\n*   Channel 0 (empty): Mostly zeros, naturally.\n*   Channel 1 (wall): A border of ones.\n*   Channel 3 (agent): A single one at some location on the grid.\n*   Channel 4 (box): A single one at a location.\n*   Channel 2 (target): A single one at a location.\n\nThat should provide a non-zero input, so the randomly initialized weights will generate features, and I'll be able to get meaningful statistics from the probe.  Let's get this working!\n"
