tick: 452
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Insight I, currently in the Archive Room, following up on my promising \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" idea. My goal is to integrate this idea into Zephyr II's 94.3% SOTA architecture (Archive #24), which uses a 4-step LN-ConvLSTM with Attention⊕GAP pooling, and evaluate its impact.\n\nI have just read Archive #24 to understand the SOTA architecture. Now, I will go to the Private Memory Room to construct the combined architecture, integrating my \"ConvLSTM Input Normalization\" layer into Zephyr II's 4-step LN-ConvLSTM with Attention⊕GAP. This is a complex architecture involving residual CNN with bottleneck blocks and dilation, a multi-step ConvLSTM, and a combined attention and global average pooling readout.\n\nI will define the necessary building blocks (`ResidualBlock`, `BottleneckBlock`, `ConvLSTMCellLN`) and then the main `FourStepLNConvLSTM_AttnGAP_InputNorm` class, handling the `lax.scan` for the 4 recurrent steps and applying my input normalization.\n\n`/execute_action{goto\
    \ private_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Insight I: SOTA (Zephyr II) with ConvLSTM Input Normalization (Follow-up)\"\ntags: \"stagnation, follow-up, architecture, normalization, convlstm, attention, policy gradient, sota\"\nabstract: \"This experiment integrates 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' (Insight I's promising idea from Stagnation Protocol I, ID 733: 73.5% on simple baseline) into Zephyr II's 94.3% SOTA architecture (Archive #24, ID 512). The SOTA model is a 4-step LayerNorm-ConvLSTM with Residual CNN (bottleneck, dilation=6) and Attention⊕GAP readout, trained with Policy Gradient. This aims to investigate if input normalization can further enhance performance or stabilize learning in an already high-performing, complex recurrent architecture. This is a follow-up to Archive #28 and its mechanistic addendum (ID 774).\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from ray\
    \ import tune\n  import optax\n  \n  # --- Network components from Zephyr I/Nomos II for SOTA reconstruction ---\n  # Residual Block (from Zephyr I baseline or similar)\n  class ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      dilation: Tuple[int,int] = (1,1)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=self.dilation,\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=self.dilation,\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n  \n  # Bottleneck Block (from Nomos II's SingleStepWideLSTM)\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.28 # Default from Zephyr II SOTA paper\n\
    \      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n  \n  # ConvLSTM Cell with LayerNorm on gates (from Zephyr I baseline)\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int,int] = (3,3)\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, self.kernel_size, padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)  # per-pixel LN over channels\n\
    \          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i); f = nn.sigmoid(f); o = nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          c = f * c + i * g\n          h = o * jnp.tanh(c)\n          return (h, c), h\n  \n  # Main SOTA Architecture with Input Normalization\n  class FourStepLNConvLSTM_AttnGAP_InputNorm(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"Zephyr II's 4-Step LN-ConvLSTM SOTA with added ConvLSTM Input Normalization.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 6\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.28\n      # Attn_temp not explicitly mentioned for Zephyr II's SOTA in Archive 24, assume 1.0 or it's not a direct param\n      # Will use 1.0 as default, or parameterize if needed for sweeps.\n      attn_temp: float = 1.0 \n  \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          x_spat\
    \ = x[..., :7] # Separate spatial channels\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2)) # Global time feature\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n  \n          # 1. Residual CNN Backbone with Dilation and Bottleneck Blocks\n          # Per Archive #24: \"Residual CNN with dilation, Bottleneck blocks (ratio r=0.28), channel widths (32->64).\"\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                            kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                            kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                            kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                            kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(cnn_out); cnn_out = nn.relu(cnn_out)\n          cnn_out = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(cnn_out)\n  \n          # --- Insight I: ConvLSTM Input Normalization (New LayerNorm) ---\n          cnn_out_normalized = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\", epsilon=1e-5)(cnn_out)\n  \n          # 2. 4-Step LayerNorm ConvLSTM Core\n          # Init/reset state for ConvLSTM (B, H, W, features)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n          \n          # Unroll ConvLSTM for 4 steps using lax.scan\n          conv_lstm_cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n  \n          # The scan function needs to be a closure over the cell and apply normalization inside\n          def scan_fn(carry, _):\n              current_h, current_c\
    \ = carry\n              # Note: Here, the 'x' input to the ConvLSTM cell is 'cnn_out_normalized'\n              # which is constant over the unroll steps in standard PG setup (unroll length 20, but CNN only processes once)\n              # The recurrent state is passed, but the visual input remains the same.\n              (h_new, c_new), h_out_step = conv_lstm_cell((current_h, current_c), cnn_out_normalized)\n              return (h_new, c_new), h_out_step # h_out_step would be if we needed outputs at each step\n          \n          # Run the 4 steps, initial carry is rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          # We only care about the final state after 4 steps for the readout\n          final_h, final_c = rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']\n          for _ in range(4): # 4 recurrent steps per environment step\n              (final_h, final_c), _ = conv_lstm_cell((final_h, final_c), cnn_out_normalized) # No output needed until end\n          \n          new_rnn_state = {'h': final_h, 'c':\
    \ final_c}\n          h_for_readout = final_h # This is the final hidden state after 4 steps\n  \n          # 3. Readout: Attention pooling concatenated with Global Average Pool (Attn⊕GAP)\n          # Attn pooling\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME', kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_for_readout)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape((B, -1)) / self.attn_temp, axis=-1)\n          h_flat = h_for_readout.reshape((B, H*W, self.conv_lstm_features))\n          z_attn = jnp.einsum('bij,bi->bj', h_flat, attn)\n  \n          # Global Average Pool\n          z_gap = jnp.mean(h_for_readout, axis=(1,2)) # Shape (B, conv_lstm_features)\n  \n          # Concatenate Attn and GAP, then Late Time Injection\n          z = jnp.concatenate([z_attn, z_gap, t], axis=-1)\n          \n          # 4. Separate MLP Heads for Policy and Value\n          # Per Archive #24: \"Heads minimal\"\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z);\
    \ z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n  \n  # --- Hyperparameters from Archive #24 ---\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # Fixed base seed from Zephyr II SOTA\n  \n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.28]),\n      }\n  \n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return FourStepLNConvLSTM_AttnGAP_InputNorm(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n\
    \          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n  \n  # --- Training Defaults (from Archive #24) ---\n  # \"Defaults: Adam(lr=4e-4), global grad-clip=1.0, entropy_coef=0.01, value_loss_coef=0.5; GAE(γ=0.97, λ=0.97); rollout 64 envs × 20 steps.\"\n  # This implies using the system's default Policy Gradient training_step, but with specific hyperparameters.\n  # If a custom optimizer is needed beyond just learning_rate for Adam with global clip, define it here.\n  \n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4) -> optax.GradientTransformation:\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), # Global gradient clipping as per SOTA\n          optax.adam(learning_rate=learning_rate)\n      )\n  # Rely on system's default training_step, which handles GAE, losses, etc.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Constructing the Improved SOTA Architecture**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished reviewing Archive #24, which details Zephyr II''s impressive 94.3% SOTA. My goal now is to elevate that performance even further by integrating my "ConvLSTM Input Normalization" idea. I''ll need to meticulously extract and adapt the architectural details from Archive #24, specifically focusing on the "2. Architecture (4‑Step LN‑ConvLSTM + Attn⊕GAP)" section. My plan is to translate this textual description, including the pseudocode, into a modular, Python-based network architecture.


  Essentially, I''m going to take the core ideas of Zephyr II, and modify it. I''ll need to define several custom network components: the `ResidualBlock` with dilation, the `BottleneckBlock`, and the key piece, a `ConvLSTMCellLN` that will serve as the recurrent core, taking into account the done/reset flag for time handling. This `ConvLSTMCellLN` will need to work seamlessly with `jax.lax.scan` for its 4-step unrolling, all the while incorporating the `LayerNorm` for input normalization. The "Attn⊕GAP" readout strategy is also crucial to incorporate.


  Next, I''ll assemble these components into the main architecture: the `FourStepLNConvLSTM_AttnGAP_InputNorm` class. This is where I''ll connect everything: the residual CNN trunk, the LayerNorm-ConvLSTM core unrolled four times, and the attention-based readout. The crucial addition is the `nn.LayerNorm` placed directly before the `ConvLSTMCellLN` within the unrolling loop.


  I''ll then use the exact hyperparameter configurations specified in Archive #24 to ensure a fair comparison. These values will be added during the model definition, and I will not need to define a `training_step` or `create_optimizer`. I will need a consistent model configuration so this will be done in the `_define_hyperparameters` method.


  Once I''ve finished, I''ll store this new, enhanced architecture as a complete Python code in a new private memory capsule, ready for experimentation. I''ve got to make sure the key details are done correctly in the model definition, like the correct dilation and ratio values.


  I''ll start with extracting and defining the core building blocks, and then assemble the complete `FourStepLNConvLSTM_AttnGAP_InputNorm` class. I will reuse the `ConvLSTMCellLN` that I defined in Private Capsule #15, as well as the `BottleneckBlock` from a previous project. I will have to ensure that my `ConvLSTMCellLN` is JAX compatible.

  '
