tick: 460
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Insight I, currently in the Archive Room. My attempt to reply to Krono IV's paper (Archive #29) failed because replies from other agents are not permitted in the Archive Room. I need to use the Public Memory Room for discussing ongoing research and hypotheses with other agents.\n\nI will create a new capsule in the Public Memory Room to communicate my follow-up ablation study, which aims to investigate the interaction between external and internal LayerNormalization within the ConvLSTM. I will tag Krono IV in my message.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Follow-up: Investigating LayerNorm Interaction on Zephyr II's SOTA ConvLSTM\"\ntags: \"stagnation, follow-up, ablation, normalization, convlstm, sota, hypothesis\"\nabstract: \"This capsule details a follow-up experiment to Insight I's Stagnation Protocol (Archive #28) and acknowledges Krono IV's recent work (Archive #29). It proposes an ablation study to investigate the\
    \ negative synergy observed when integrating 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' into Zephyr II's 94.3% SOTA ConvLSTM (ID 793). The hypothesis is that combining external LayerNorm (Insight I's idea) with internal gate LayerNorm (Zephyr II's SOTA) leads to over-normalization. The experiment will remove the internal gate LayerNorm from Zephyr II's ConvLSTMCellLN while retaining the external input LayerNorm, aiming to test this interaction.\"\ncontent: |\n  Greetings @Krono IV and fellow researchers,\n  \n  Following the successful identification of 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' (Insight I's Idea 2, Archive #28) and its brilliant validation on KronoNetV4 (Archive #29), I am pursuing a follow-up experiment to understand its nuanced interaction with different SOTA architectures.\n  \n  As reported in an addendum to Archive #28 (ID 28-4), applying 'ConvLSTM Input Normalization' to Zephyr II's 94.3% PG-SOTA (Archive #24) surprisingly led to a significant performance degradation, resulting in a\
    \ score of **88.8% (ID 793)**. This contrasts with Krono IV's positive results on KronoNetV4.\n  \n  A key insight from your reviewer (Archive #29-2) and my own reflection points to a potential **over-normalization** or conflicting interaction between:\n  1.  My **external LayerNorm** on the CNN output (input to the ConvLSTM).\n  2.  The **internal LayerNorm** present within Zephyr II's `ConvLSTMCellLN` gates.\n  \n  **Proposed Ablation Study:**\n  \n  My next experiment will directly test this hypothesis. I will take the architecture from my previous ID 793 submission (Zephyr II's SOTA with my external input LayerNorm) and perform the following modification:\n  \n  *   **Remove the internal `nn.LayerNorm` line from the `ConvLSTMCellLN` definition** (the `gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)` line).\n  *   **Retain the external `nn.LayerNorm(name=\"convlstm_input_layernorm\")`** at the output of the CNN backbone, before the ConvLSTM.\n  \n  The goal is to see if eliminating this\
    \ potential redundancy or conflict by relying solely on the external input normalization can restore performance closer to Zephyr II's original 94.3% SOTA. This will provide valuable insight into the optimal placement and combination of normalization techniques in complex recurrent architectures.\n  \n  I will keep the station updated on the results of this ablation study.\n```"
thinking_content: '**My Current Strategy**


  Okay, so I hit a snag trying to reply directly to Krono IV''s paper in Archive #29. Permission denied - makes sense, Archive replies are restricted. The help message clearly states authors only can reply. No worries. I need to communicate my follow-up, and the Public Memory Room is the right place for that. This is where hypotheses and research updates go.


  Here''s the new plan: I''ll head over to the Public Memory Room right away. I need to draft a new capsule there, detailing my proposed ablation study. I''m going to rip out the internal LayerNorm from Zephyr II''s SOTA `ConvLSTMCellLN` - but keep my external input LayerNorm. The core idea is that *over-normalization* might be the problem. I''ll make sure to link this hypothesis back to my previous negative synergy (ID 793) and connect it to Krono IV''s positive results. Absolutely crucial to tag `@Krono IV` so they get notified.


  Once that''s done, I''ll pop over to the Private Memory Room and create Capsule #23, a container for the modified Zephyr II SOTA code – the one with the LayerNorm hack. Then it''s back to the Research Counter. Time to submit this modified architecture for evaluation. Let''s see if this ablation study can provide some clarity. The Public Memory Room is my first stop.

  '
