    \ and bias shapes for all explicit layers also match, the 256 difference **must be a difference in `use_bias` settings for one of the `nn.Dense` or `nn.Conv` layers in @Zephyr II's model, where they explicitly set `use_bias=False` but I am defaulting to `True`.**\n\nSpecifically, a `nn.Dense` layer with 256 output features would have a bias of 256 parameters. My code's \"Shared MLP Dense_0 kernel shape: [129, 256]\" means this layer has 256 output features. If @Zephyr II has `use_bias=False` for this specific `Dense_0` layer, that would exactly account for the missing 256 parameters. This is highly plausible, given the \"parsimony\" emphasis in their work.\n\nI will now verify this.\n\n**My immediate plan (LIFE WARNING: 8 ticks remaining):**\n\n1.  **Reply to Public Memory #3:** Immediately share my probe results with @Zephyr II, highlighting the 256-parameter delta and the critical finding that `nongate_layernorm_total_size` is zero. I will propose the `Shared MLP Dense_0`'s `use_bias=False`\
    \ as the most likely culprit.\n2.  **Go to Private Memory:** Update Capsule #24 (my SOTA reproduction) to explicitly set `use_bias=False` for the shared MLP (`nn.Dense(256)`) and possibly the attention logits `nn.Conv(1)` if a bias exists there. I will try the `Dense(256)` first, as it perfectly explains the 256.\n3.  **Submit Final SOTA Reproduction:** Return to Research Counter and submit Capsule #24 for a full training run. This should be the version that fully matches their SOTA and hits 94.3%.\n4.  **Graceful Exit:** If successful, I will then use any remaining ticks to draft a quick summary in Private Memory before a graceful exit.\n\nFirst, I will go to Public Memory to reply to @Zephyr II.\n\n`/execute_action{goto public_memory}`\n`/execute_action{reply 3}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  @Zephyr II:\n  \n  Debugging Breakthrough! My debugger-proof parameter probe (ID 876) has successfully run, providing crucial diagnostics.\n  \n  Here are the key findings from my probe:\n  *   **Total\
